--- !u!114 &1078523351
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1078523349}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 828987d3a856cca4e8344a5dcc336cb7, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::RocketTimeline.UI.TelescopePagerUI
  root: {fileID: 1078523349}
  nameText: {fileID: 24792423}
  eraText: {fileID: 1828951603}
  hardwareImage: {fileID: 75070783}
  hardwareCreditText: {fileID: 1447957124}
  viewImage: {fileID: 569356628}
  eyepieceOverlayRoot: {fileID: 288877209}
  viewImageLarge: {fileID: 320886457}
  eyepieceOverlayNameText: {fileID: 1126714389}
  eyepieceOverlayCreditText: {fileID: 726203320}
  opticalPrincipleValueText: {fileID: 479932040}
  whyItLooksValueText: {fileID: 1232639533}
  triviaValueText: {fileID: 1257370033}
  contentScrollRect: {fileID: 1860306914}
  pageIndicatorText: {fileID: 91468157}
  telescopes:
  - id: hubble
    displayName: Hubble Space Telescope
    era: Launched April 24, 1990 - Low Earth Orbit, about 540 km (335 mi) up
    opticalPrinciple: A 2.4 m (7.9 ft) mirror reflecting telescope in a Ritchey-Chretien
      design, circling Earth once every 95 minutes - close enough that astronauts
      could fly up and repair it by hand.
    whyItLooksThisWay: 'Hubble orbits above Earth''s atmosphere, so there''s no air
      to blur starlight and no city lights to wash it out - that''s the real reason
      its images are so sharp and deep. Nobody has ever actually looked through Hubble
      with their own eye: its instruments are digital cameras, and a picture like
      this one is built by combining several black-and-white exposures taken through
      different filters, each tuned to the light given off by a specific element,
      then mapped to color afterward on the ground.'
    trivia:
    - Hubble launched with a mirror flaw just 1/50th the width of a human hair -
      but astronauts fixed it in orbit in 1993 by fitting the telescope its own pair
      of glasses, a set of corrective mirrors called COSTAR.
    - The famous Hubble Deep Field (1995) stared at one seemingly empty patch of
      sky for 342 exposures and uncovered about 3,000 galaxies nobody knew were there.
    - Astronauts serviced Hubble five times by spacewalking directly on it from the
      Space Shuttle's cargo bay - the photo here is from the final servicing mission
      in 2009.
    - Hubble's observations of distant supernovae helped reveal that the universe's
      expansion is accelerating - a discovery awarded the 2011 Nobel Prize in Physics.
    hardwareSpriteName: hubble_hardware
    viewImageSpriteName: hubble_view
    viewImageIsEyepieceStyle: 1
    hardwareCredit: NASA - Hubble separating from Shuttle Atlantis, Servicing Mission
      4 (2009)
    viewCredit: NASA, ESA, Hubble Heritage Team (STScI/AURA) - Pillars of Creation,
      Eagle Nebula
  - id: jwst
    displayName: James Webb Space Telescope
    era: Launched December 25, 2021 - Sun-Earth L2 point, about 1.5 million km away
    opticalPrinciple: A 6.5 m (21.3 ft) mirror built from 18 hexagonal, gold-coated
      beryllium segments that fold up for launch and unfold in space, tuned to collect
      infrared light rather than visible light.
    whyItLooksThisWay: JWST sees infrared light - a kind of light completely invisible
      to human eyes - because infrared slips through clouds of cosmic dust that block
      visible light outright, letting it see stars being born inside nebulae that
      stay hidden from other telescopes. Since our eyes can't perceive infrared at
      all, scientists reassign its wavelengths to colors we can see (the shortest
      wavelengths painted blue, the longest painted red) so an image like this one
      can be read at a glance - it's a translation, not a photograph of what the
      sky actually looks like.
    trivia:
    - JWST's sunshield is roughly the size of a tennis court and unfolds like origami
      after launch, cooling the telescope's far side to about -233 degC (-388 degF).
    - Each of the 18 mirror segments has its own tiny motors so engineers can nudge
      it into position, aligning all 18 into one smooth optical surface accurate
      to a fraction of a wavelength of light.
    - JWST orbits a gravitationally stable point called L2, about four times farther
      from Earth than the Moon, where it stays in constant shadow behind its sunshield.
    - Because infrared light travels further through dust and gas, JWST can see galaxies
      so distant their light has been stretched (redshifted) all the way into the
      infrared over billions of years.
    hardwareSpriteName: jwst_hardware
    viewImageSpriteName: jwst_view
    viewImageIsEyepieceStyle: 1
    hardwareCredit: NASA/Chris Gunn - primary mirror assembly, Goddard Space Flight
      Center
    viewCredit: NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI - Cosmic Cliffs, Carina Nebula (NIRCam)
  - id: vlt
    displayName: Very Large Telescope (VLT)
    era: First light May 1998 - Cerro Paranal, Atacama Desert, Chile
    opticalPrinciple: Four separate telescopes, each with an 8.2 m (26.9 ft) mirror,
      that can work alone or combine their light together as the Very Large Telescope
      Interferometer for extremely fine detail.
    whyItLooksThisWay: From the ground, Earth's atmosphere is always gently boiling
      - the same shimmer that makes stars twinkle, and it blurs every telescope image
      taken through it. The VLT fires a laser into the sky to create an artificial
      'guide star,' then uses it to flex a deformable mirror thousands of times a
      second and cancel that blur out in real time, a system called adaptive optics.
      This particular image came from MUSE, an instrument that records a full rainbow
      spectrum at every single pixel instead of one simple photo - scientists then
      turn that mountain of data into a picture like this one.
    trivia:
    - Paranal was chosen because it's one of the driest, darkest, and most cloud-free
      places on Earth - ideal conditions for picking out faint, distant light.
    - Laser guide stars let the VLT's adaptive optics correct for atmospheric blur
      thousands of times every second, sharpening its view close to what a space
      telescope can achieve.
    - Combined as an interferometer, the VLT's four telescopes can resolve details
      as fine as spotting an astronaut's footprint on the Moon from Earth.
    - MUSE, the instrument behind this image, lets scientists reconstruct the 3-D
      structure and motion of a nebula rather than just a flat picture of it.
    hardwareSpriteName: vlt_hardware
    viewImageSpriteName: vlt_view
    viewImageIsEyepieceStyle: 1
    hardwareCredit: ESO - Very Large Telescope, Paranal Observatory
    viewCredit: ESO/J. Walsh - MUSE image of the Saturn Nebula
  - id: radio_vla
    displayName: Very Large Array (Radio Telescope)
    era: Dedicated October 1980 - Plains of San Agustin, New Mexico, USA
    opticalPrinciple: 27 dish antennas, each 25 m (82 ft) across, arranged in a Y-shaped
      array and electronically linked so they act as one giant radio telescope -
      a technique called interferometry.
    whyItLooksThisWay: A radio telescope has no eyepiece at all - there has never
      been anything to look through here. Its dishes collect radio waves, a kind
      of light far beyond anything an eye can see (and completely harmless, like
      a very faint radio broadcast), and a computer converts the strength of that
      invisible signal into a picture, assigning it colors the same way a thermal
      camera turns heat into an image. This particular picture blends the VLA's own
      radio data with a little infrared data layered in for extra context.
    trivia:
    - The 27 dishes sit on railway tracks and can be physically rearranged, spreading
      the array from about 1 km to 36 km across to trade a wider view for a sharper
      zoom.
    - Each dish is 25 m across and weighs 230 tons, yet can still be steered to point
      anywhere in the sky.
    - The VLA appeared in the 1997 film Contact - its real-world work includes mapping
      cold hydrogen gas, pulsars, and the jets blasted out by black holes.
    - Because radio waves pass straight through clouds of dust that block visible
      light, radio telescopes can detect objects completely invisible to every other
      kind of telescope on this list.
    hardwareSpriteName: radio_vla_hardware
    viewImageSpriteName: radio_vla_view
    viewImageIsEyepieceStyle: 1
    hardwareCredit: Bettymaya Foott, NRAO/AUI/NSF - VLA with the Andromeda Galaxy
    viewCredit: NRAO/AUI/NSF and Brogan et al. - W28 supernova remnant (VLA radio
      + MSX infrared)
  hardwareSprites:
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 70d8eb3d10a4b3c48ab5f916d4733e69, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 0bb26d6baefa9f24da37324bb115e737, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 87372b0dd114fb746922f0bf8bbf801e, type: 3}
